#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "bb5306eb-02c5-52a3-b7b0-92610059b065" :question/math-academy-id "e-15064" :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/f5018f5963c09ade7244ff19220fbce9.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n$x = (u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1$.\n\nFor the side $S_1$, we have $v = 0$ and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq u \\leq 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= ((u + v)^{2}, \\frac{u}{v + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((u + 0)^{2}, \\frac{u}{0 + 1}) \\\\\n&= (u^{2}, u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n$y^2=x$\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$.\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\nT: (0, 0) → ((0 + 0)^{2}, \\frac{0}{0 + 1}) = (0, 0); T: (1, 0) → ((1 + 0)^{2}, \\frac{1}{0 + 1}) = (1, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/0bfa665ade1f4ccd9b7293a0d1b0370e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/id #uuid "d08f8e35-c4e7-5eef-aca5-175585760433" :question/math-academy-id "e-15065" :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/bb3f4499d045cb59b5d13942268c59c2.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v)$.\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta$. So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1$, where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1$. Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (u(0), u(u + 0)); = (0, u^{2}), \\mid 0 \\le u \\le 1$.\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2$. Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1$. Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (1(v), 1(1 + v)); = (v, 1 + v), \\mid 0 \\le v \\le 1$.\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3$. Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0$. Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (u(1), u(u + 1)); = (u, u^{2} + u), \\mid 1 \\ge u \\ge 0$.\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4$. Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0$. Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (0v, 0(0 + v)); = (0, 0), \\mid 1 \\ge v \\ge 0$.\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/29a5c447957277442ef4784d7252b080.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "f35926af-6c5f-537f-ab22-ec2d62995267" :question/math-academy-id "e-15066" :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/eb83daf1d6411acc9b374a6918e705d5.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3$.\n\nFor the side $S_3$, we have $v=1$ and\n\n$$\n1 \\geq u \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3v, v^{2} - 3u) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3(1), 1^{2} - 3u) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3, 1 - 3u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/7a1dec23fac3c206ac36d01957cf50c7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ec73a2b5-8f4a-5418-983d-b35013387844" :question/math-academy-id "e-15087" :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/301e42965375d20de3dae4fc94252bcb.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n$x = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0$.\n\nFind the function $x(t)$." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4$.\n\nFor the side $S_4$, we have $u = 0$ and\n\n$$\n1 \\geq v \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u - v^{2}, 2v) \\\\\n&= (0 - v^{2}, 2v) \\\\\n&= (-v^{2}, 2v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0659196-7133-54fe-8b61-0453676189b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153816} {:answer/id #uuid "c3533e64-d456-5711-afb3-7f42cdb317e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153817} {:answer/id #uuid "0da0b259-3fa0-543f-a443-ea24739aef3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153818} {:answer/id #uuid "30653f6a-0b46-5f44-8a79-b093ef6a9253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153819} {:answer/id #uuid "558206b9-a9c5-57df-b604-173cf6f2ea85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30653f6a-0b46-5f44-8a79-b093ef6a9253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153819} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7004c7f-dc8d-5227-b366-6acfa28dce70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153815}] :question/id #uuid "91008bb7-2af5-5c56-89d5-09e110f3df57" :question/math-academy-id "q-160353" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = v, y = u(1 + v^{2})$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a80b528d-0d08-5e82-b336-a72441e07f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153840} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ba386-8f63-50f7-b83d-e8ca4a4ac0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153841} {:answer/id #uuid "f89cbcce-d7bd-56e6-be52-17a310719381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153842} {:answer/id #uuid "28de49a8-b1bc-56f8-8004-6fbd95b12e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153843} {:answer/id #uuid "4b015fde-f6ba-5b61-ae66-1f25ba50a2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a80b528d-0d08-5e82-b336-a72441e07f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153840} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e22cff-2c67-59d6-8253-4950127753b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153839}] :question/id #uuid "c81115ea-cfb5-58ec-bdf5-a51e72b6004d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160605.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = u - v, y = u(1 + v)$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n$x =-t, y = y (t), 1 \\ge t \\ge 0$.\n\nFind the function $y(t)$."}]}
